I think the repro above needs a bit of fixing. I'm able to repro the data race failure even when modifying that `TestUnqualifiedIntSizeRace` test like this:
```
-	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET default_int_size = 8"}`))
-	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET default_int_size = 4"}`))
+	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET application_name = 'a'"}`))
+	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET application_name = 'b'"}`))
```

Based on the output i get, it looks like the failure is because of the `_ = stmtBuff` read that's at the end of the test.

If I remove the stmtBuf entirely and change the test to:
```
func TestUnqualifiedIntSizeRace(t *testing.T) {
	defer leaktest.AfterTest(t)()
	defer log.Scope(t).Close(t)

	ctx := context.Background()

	const (
		// select generates 10 rows of results which the test retrieves using ExecPortal
		selectStmt = "SELECT generate_series(1, 10)"

		// stmtBufMaxLen is the maximum length the statement buffer should be during
		// execution
		stmtBufMaxLen = 2

		// The name of the portal, used to get a handle on the statement buffer
		portalName = "C_1"
	)

	s, _, _ := serverutils.StartServer(t, base.TestServerArgs{
		Knobs: base.TestingKnobs{
			SQLExecutor: &sql.ExecutorTestingKnobs{},
		},
		Insecure: true,
	})
	defer s.Stopper().Stop(ctx)

	// Connect to the cluster via the PGWire client.
	p, err := pgtest.NewPGTest(ctx, s.SQLAddr(), security.RootUser)
	require.NoError(t, err)

	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET default_int_size = 8"}`))
	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Query {"String": "SET default_int_size = 4"}`))
	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Parse {"Query": "SELECT generate_series(1, 10)"}`))
	require.NoError(t, p.SendOneLine(`Bind {"DestinationPortal": "C_1"}`))

	// wait for ready
	for i := 0; i < 2; i++ {
		until := pgtest.ParseMessages("ReadyForQuery")
		_, err = p.Until(false /* keepErrMsg */, until...)
		require.NoError(t, err)
	}
}
```

then i do get the DefaultIntSize race with `make stressrace PKG=./pkg/sql TESTS=TestUnqualifiedIntSizeRace`.
```
WARNING: DATA RACE
Write at 0x00c0010084cc by goroutine 350:
  github.com/cockroachdb/cockroach/pkg/sql.(*sessionDataMutator).SetDefaultIntSize()
      /Users/rafiss/go/src/github.com/cockroachdb/cockroach/pkg/sql/exec_util.go:2645 +0x1f5
```

I'm able to fix the race with this patch, which adds locks to SessionDataStack and using atomc.Load/Store for default int size.

(**probably for release-21.1 and before, we should backport the atomic.Load/Store change**)

```
diff --git a/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go b/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
index fb0a615616..ec636af8bb 100644
--- a/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
+++ b/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
@@ -625,7 +625,8 @@ func (h ConnectionHandler) GetUnqualifiedIntSize() *types.T {
     if h.ex != nil {
         // The executor will be nil in certain testing situations where
         // no server is actually present.
-        size = h.ex.sessionData().DefaultIntSize
+        sd := h.ex.sessionData()
+        size = atomic.LoadInt32(&sd.DefaultIntSize)
     }
     return parser.NakedIntTypeFromDefaultIntSize(size)
 }

diff --git a/pkg/sql/exec_util.go b/pkg/sql/exec_util.go
index efa7737e76..49399b3fb5 100644
--- a/pkg/sql/exec_util.go
+++ b/pkg/sql/exec_util.go
@@ -2642,7 +2643,7 @@ func (m *sessionDataMutator) SetTemporarySchemaIDForDatabase(dbID uint32, tempSc
 }

 func (m *sessionDataMutator) SetDefaultIntSize(size int32) {
-    m.data.DefaultIntSize = size
+    atomic.StoreInt32(&m.data.DefaultIntSize, size)
 }

diff --git a/pkg/sql/sessiondata/session_data.go b/pkg/sql/sessiondata/session_data.go
index f59ac9d894..74f56055a8 100644
--- a/pkg/sql/sessiondata/session_data.go
+++ b/pkg/sql/sessiondata/session_data.go
@@ -201,6 +202,7 @@ func (s *SessionData) GetTemporarySchemaIDForDb(dbID uint32) (uint32, bool) {
 // affects the top of the stack.
 // There is always guaranteed to be one element in the stack.
 type Stack struct {
+    syncutil.RWMutex // and add s.Lock() / defer s.Unlock() to all the method.
     // Use an internal variable to prevent abstraction leakage.
     stack []*SessionData
 }
```

It's also fixed just by making `GetUnqualifiedIntSize()` stop reading sessiondata (as @otan suggested)
```
diff --git a/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go b/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
index fb0a615616..ec636af8bb 100644
--- a/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
+++ b/pkg/sql/conn_executor.go
@@ -625,7 +625,8 @@ func (h ConnectionHandler) GetUnqualifiedIntSize() *types.T {
     if h.ex != nil {
         // The executor will be nil in certain testing situations where
         // no server is actually present.
-        size = h.ex.sessionData().DefaultIntSize
     }
     return parser.NakedIntTypeFromDefaultIntSize(size)
 }
```

By the way, I noticed this comment in pgwire/conn.go which basically spells out the fact that there already is a race condition.
```
	// We need an intSizer, which we're ultimately going to get from the
	// authenticator once authentication succeeds (because it will actually be a
	// ConnectionHandler). Until then, we unfortunately still need some intSizer
	// because we technically might enqueue parsed statements in the statement
	// buffer even before authentication succeeds (because we need this go routine
	// to keep reading from the network connection while authentication is in
	// progress in order to react to the connection closing).
	var intSizer unqualifiedIntSizer = fixedIntSizer{size: types.Int}
```

So to summarize:
- for 21.1 and before, we should backport a change to make DefaultIntSize get accessed with `atomic.Load/Store`
- for 21.2 we either have to either:
  1. add a mutex around all SessionDataStack operations AND use atomic.Load/Store
  2. stop using a session setting for default_int_size. @otan could you explain a bit more why it's backward compatible to start ignoring the session setting?
  3. (haven't investigated yet) change pgwire/conn.go to stop using that `fixedIntSizer` and instead have it wait for the real `intSizer` to be sent over to it from the background goroutine. but seems like that would cause the pgwire `serveImpl` loop to block when we don't want it to.
